1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,758
Every evening, Evan sat at the same bus stop.

2
00:00:03,758 --> 00:00:07,517
He left enough room for someone beside him, then

3
00:00:07,517 --> 00:00:11,275
placed his bag in the empty space.

4
00:00:11,695 --> 00:00:16,033
He had moved to the city eight months earlier.

5
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:20,371
Work started early. His friends lived elsewhere. Conversations arrived

6
00:00:20,371 --> 00:00:24,710
through a screen, usually after he had fallen asleep.

7
00:00:25,130 --> 00:00:27,568
There were people he could message. He opened their

8
00:00:27,568 --> 00:00:30,006
names, considered a greeting, and closed the app. Surely

9
00:00:30,006 --> 00:00:32,444
they would call if they wanted to hear from

10
00:00:32,444 --> 00:00:34,882
him.

11
00:00:35,302 --> 00:00:39,613
Across the street, apartment windows held fragments of other

12
00:00:39,613 --> 00:00:43,924
lives. A hand passing a plate. Two heads bent

13
00:00:43,924 --> 00:00:48,236
over a game. From outside, belonging looked effortless.

14
00:00:48,656 --> 00:00:52,111
His own dinner took six minutes to heat. He

15
00:00:52,111 --> 00:00:55,567
ate standing up, listening to a podcast so the

16
00:00:55,567 --> 00:00:59,023
kitchen would sound as though someone else was there.

17
00:00:59,443 --> 00:01:03,410
On Friday, an old friend posted a photograph from

18
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:07,377
a birthday dinner. Evan recognized the faces. He had

19
00:01:07,377 --> 00:01:11,343
never mentioned that he could have visited that weekend.

20
00:01:11,763 --> 00:01:14,927
He put the phone down, hurt by an invitation

21
00:01:14,927 --> 00:01:18,091
that had never been offered. Then he picked it

22
00:01:18,091 --> 00:01:21,255
up again, hoping the next notification would change the

23
00:01:21,255 --> 00:01:24,418
evening.

24
00:01:24,838 --> 00:01:28,484
It was not that he disliked being alone. Some

25
00:01:28,484 --> 00:01:32,129
mornings, solitude felt generous. What wore him down was

26
00:01:32,129 --> 00:01:35,775
having nobody nearby who expected him to arrive.

27
00:01:36,195 --> 00:01:40,034
One wet Tuesday, a woman stopped beside the bench,

28
00:01:40,034 --> 00:01:43,873
carrying a broken lamp. Evan moved his bag. She

29
00:01:43,873 --> 00:01:47,712
thanked him and set the lamp between her shoes.

30
00:01:48,132 --> 00:01:51,209
Rain drummed against the shelter. The community center held

31
00:01:51,209 --> 00:01:54,285
a repair table on Thursdays. They would see whether

32
00:01:54,285 --> 00:01:57,362
the lamp could be saved.

33
00:01:57,782 --> 00:02:00,911
He knew the building. He had walked past its

34
00:02:00,911 --> 00:02:04,040
bright windows many times, imagining that everyone inside already

35
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:07,169
knew everyone else. He had never checked whether that

36
00:02:07,169 --> 00:02:10,298
was true.

37
00:02:10,718 --> 00:02:13,707
You could come along, she said. He answered that

38
00:02:13,707 --> 00:02:16,697
Thursdays were difficult. It was not entirely a lie.

39
00:02:16,697 --> 00:02:19,686
After work, leaving the apartment again really did feel

40
00:02:19,686 --> 00:02:22,676
difficult.

41
00:02:23,096 --> 00:02:26,977
That night, he checked his phone several times. Nobody

42
00:02:26,977 --> 00:02:30,859
had invited him anywhere. Then he remembered the lamp,

43
00:02:30,859 --> 00:02:34,741
the woman, and the invitation he had already declined.

44
00:02:35,161 --> 00:02:38,615
He drew his own little pattern: expect rejection, stay

45
00:02:38,615 --> 00:02:42,069
out of reach, receive no new invitation. It described

46
00:02:42,069 --> 00:02:45,522
his recent evenings, not a rule about everybody who

47
00:02:45,522 --> 00:02:48,976
feels alone.

48
00:02:49,396 --> 00:02:52,377
There were reasons his life had become small: a

49
00:02:52,377 --> 00:02:55,358
move, long hours, distance, money. Those things were real.

50
00:02:55,358 --> 00:02:58,339
So was the small amount of room he could

51
00:02:58,339 --> 00:03:01,320
still make.

52
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:05,118
He messaged his old friend: I miss seeing you.

53
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:08,497
Could we talk on Sunday? His thumb hovered before

54
00:03:08,497 --> 00:03:11,875
sending. A specific question felt much more exposed than

55
00:03:11,875 --> 00:03:15,254
a reaction emoji.

56
00:03:15,674 --> 00:03:19,695
The reply arrived the next morning. Sunday was full.

57
00:03:19,695 --> 00:03:23,716
Evan felt the familiar drop in his stomach. Then

58
00:03:23,716 --> 00:03:27,737
another message followed: What about Tuesday, after dinner?

59
00:03:28,157 --> 00:03:30,830
He put Tuesday on the calendar. It was a

60
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:33,503
call, not a new social life. Still, it was

61
00:03:33,503 --> 00:03:36,176
a real arrangement with a time and another person

62
00:03:36,176 --> 00:03:38,849
inside it.

63
00:03:39,269 --> 00:03:42,642
On Thursday, he walked toward the community center without

64
00:03:42,642 --> 00:03:46,015
promising to stay. Through the window, he saw three

65
00:03:46,015 --> 00:03:49,388
people, a kettle, and a table covered with ordinary

66
00:03:49,388 --> 00:03:52,760
broken things.

67
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:55,659
He stood outside long enough for the door to

68
00:03:55,659 --> 00:03:58,138
open toward him. The woman with the lamp recognized

69
00:03:58,138 --> 00:04:00,616
his coat. Good timing, she said. Could you hold

70
00:04:00,616 --> 00:04:03,095
this?

71
00:04:03,515 --> 00:04:06,212
She handed him a paper shade while someone checked

72
00:04:06,212 --> 00:04:08,908
the cable. Suddenly there was a reason for his

73
00:04:08,908 --> 00:04:11,604
hands to be there. He did not need an

74
00:04:11,604 --> 00:04:14,301
impressive introduction.

75
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:18,107
A volunteer asked how he took his tea. Evan

76
00:04:18,107 --> 00:04:21,493
answered, then asked the same question back. It was

77
00:04:21,493 --> 00:04:24,880
a small enough conversation to manage.

78
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:29,119
The lamp did not light that evening. They needed

79
00:04:29,119 --> 00:04:32,939
a part. The woman wrote its name, and somebody

80
00:04:32,939 --> 00:04:36,759
suggested trying again next Thursday. Another Thursday.

81
00:04:37,179 --> 00:04:41,027
He replaced his earlier diagram with something smaller: a

82
00:04:41,027 --> 00:04:44,876
specific invitation, a shared activity, another meeting. Not a

83
00:04:44,876 --> 00:04:48,725
guarantee of friendship. Just a few places where familiarity

84
00:04:48,725 --> 00:04:52,573
could grow.

85
00:04:52,993 --> 00:04:56,813
Over tea, he nearly reached for his phone whenever

86
00:04:56,813 --> 00:05:00,633
the conversation paused. This time, he left it in

87
00:05:00,633 --> 00:05:04,453
his pocket. A pause was not a failure.

88
00:05:04,873 --> 00:05:07,853
On the bus home, nobody knew that anything had

89
00:05:07,853 --> 00:05:10,834
changed. He had not become outgoing. He had held

90
00:05:10,834 --> 00:05:13,815
a lampshade, learned two names, and agreed to bring

91
00:05:13,815 --> 00:05:16,796
some tape.

92
00:05:17,216 --> 00:05:20,473
The following Thursday, he was exhausted. He considered staying

93
00:05:20,473 --> 00:05:23,729
home and sending nothing. Instead, he wrote that he

94
00:05:23,729 --> 00:05:26,986
could not make it and asked whether they needed

95
00:05:26,986 --> 00:05:30,242
tape next week.

96
00:05:30,662 --> 00:05:33,684
They did. The reply was brief. Nobody demanded an

97
00:05:33,684 --> 00:05:36,705
explanation or treated his absence as a betrayal. Returning

98
00:05:36,705 --> 00:05:39,727
did not require pretending that he would never miss

99
00:05:39,727 --> 00:05:42,748
an evening.

100
00:05:43,168 --> 00:05:45,783
When he came back, the lamp worked. Warm light

101
00:05:45,783 --> 00:05:48,399
opened across the scratched table. The woman waved him

102
00:05:48,399 --> 00:05:51,014
over and asked him to show a newcomer where

103
00:05:51,014 --> 00:05:53,629
the tea was.

104
00:05:54,049 --> 00:05:56,705
A month later, Evan sat at the bus stop

105
00:05:56,705 --> 00:05:59,360
again. He moved his bag before anyone asked. When

106
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:02,016
a familiar coat appeared in the rain, he raised

107
00:06:02,016 --> 00:06:04,672
a hand.

108
00:06:05,092 --> 00:06:07,989
His old friends were still far away. Some messages

109
00:06:07,989 --> 00:06:10,885
still went unanswered. He was learning to ask without

110
00:06:10,885 --> 00:06:13,782
turning every busy person into proof that he did

111
00:06:13,782 --> 00:06:16,679
not matter.

112
00:06:17,099 --> 00:06:20,106
There were lonely evenings afterward. He could name them

113
00:06:20,106 --> 00:06:23,113
more honestly now. He also knew that one difficult

114
00:06:23,113 --> 00:06:26,120
night did not erase the people he was slowly

115
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:29,127
getting to know.

116
00:06:29,547 --> 00:06:32,470
Connection was not something he could manufacture alone. Other

117
00:06:32,470 --> 00:06:35,392
people had to meet him halfway. He could make

118
00:06:35,392 --> 00:06:38,315
that easier by letting them see when, where, and

119
00:06:38,315 --> 00:06:41,238
how to find him.

120
00:06:41,658 --> 00:06:44,595
He had not filled every empty seat. He had

121
00:06:44,595 --> 00:06:47,533
stopped keeping all of them unavailable. Beside him, there

122
00:06:47,533 --> 00:06:50,470
was room for someone, and this time, his bag

123
00:06:50,470 --> 00:06:53,407
stayed on the floor.
